# Stage 1 with the neutron's elastic scattering inactivated as well - the diagnostic column,
# and the first time it has been possible to produce one for a neutron.
#
# **RUN THIS THROUGH ref\b1neutron\run.bat**, like `stage1_neutron.mac` beside it and for the
# same reason: `EnableNeutronGeneralProcess` has no UI command, so the configuration in which
# `hadElastic` is a process of the neutron's own - and therefore something
# `/process/inactivate` can name - only exists in that binary. docs/RISK.md V60.
#
# WHAT THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN THE TWO COLUMNS IS. With the general process ON, elastic,
# inelastic and capture are sub-processes of one object and `/process/inactivate hadElastic`
# names nothing a neutron has, so the "what is this process worth" measurement every other
# species in this directory has was unavailable for the neutron. With it off, this column
# leaves the neutron with `Transportation`, `Decay`, `nCapture` and `nKiller` - so the
# difference from `stage1_neutron.mac` is what a 100 MeV neutron's elastic scattering is worth
# to B1's scoring volume, which for a neutron is most of what a dose is: the recoil protons and
# oxygen nuclei an elastic scatter makes are what deposits, and the neutron itself deposits
# nothing.
#
# `nCapture` is left active so that the column differs from its partner by ONE process. A
# thermal neutron in water captures on hydrogen and the 2.22 MeV gamma is the deposit; at
# 100 MeV that channel is reached only after the neutron has been slowed by the elastic
# scattering this macro switches off, so the number is expected to be small rather than zero.

/run/initialize
/control/verbose 0
/run/verbose 1

/process/inactivate neutronInelastic
/process/inactivate hadElastic

# The stage, recorded by what RAN. docs/RISK.md V43.
/particle/select neutron
/particle/process/dump

/gun/particle neutron
/gun/energy 100 MeV

/run/printProgress 100000
/run/beamOn 500000
